   133  /********************************************************************
   134
   135                    ISOMORPHIC TRANSFORMATIONS
   136
   137  *********************************************************************
   138
   139      In this context, an isomorphic transformation is a proj PJ
   140      object returning the same kind of coordinates that it
   141      receives, i.e. a transformation from angular to angular or
   142      linear to linear coordinates.
   143
   144      The degrees-to-radians operation is an example of the former,
   145      while the latter is typical for most of the datum shift
   146      operations used in geodesy, e.g. the Helmert 7-parameter shift.
   147
   148      Isomorphic transformations trips up the pj_inv/pj_fwd
   149      functions, which try to check input sanity and scale output to
   150      internal proj units, under the assumption that if input is of
   151      angular type, then output must be of linear (or vice versa).
   152
   153      Hence, to avoid having pj_inv/pj_fwd stomping on output (or
   154      choking on input), we need a way to tell them that they should
   155      accept whatever is being handed to them.
   156
   157      The P->left and P->right elements indicate isomorphism.
   158
   159      For classic proj style projections, they both have the
   160      value PJ_IO_UNITS_CLASSIC (default initialization), indicating
   161      that the forward driver expects angular input coordinates, and
   162      provides linear output coordinates.
   163
   164      Newer projections may set P->left and P->right to either
   165      PJ_IO_UNITS_METERS, PJ_IO_UNITS_RADIANS or PJ_IO_UNITS_ANY,
   166      to indicate their I/O style.
   167
   168      For the forward driver, left indicates input coordinate
   169      type, while right indicates output coordinate type.
   170
   171      For the inverse driver, left indicates output coordinate
   172      type, while right indicates input coordinate type.
   173
   174  *********************************************************************/
   175
